\section configuration_overview AlamDMC configuration overview

\anchor desc_alamdmc_format
Configuration files of the `alamDMC` program have a fixed format. The files are read and interpreted by the program according to Fortran formatted input rules, which implies that:
- the files are processed line by line,
- empty lines are typically interpreted as containing 'zero' of the type being read,
- tabulators are not allowed as blank characters.

\note Please make sure that AlamDMC config files do not contain tabulators.

\anchor desc_alamdmc_comments
Additionally, the configuration files can include comments that are ignored by the program. A comment starts with `#` and spans till the end of the line. A comment may start at the beginning of the line or after a data field.
\par Example:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~{.cfg}
# This is a comment
6           # This is a comment that follows a data field.
 # Beware, this comment begins at 2nd character, so this line contains (empty) data!
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

\note If a comment does not start at the first position in a line, the line is interpreted as it contained a data field.

\note There are special rules for comments in certain elements of the configuration file. See \ref range_option.

Generally, the user is supposed to provide correct configuration files. Although the program attempts some rudimentary detection of errors and mistakes in the config files, the users are advised not to rely on that feature. 

The configuration file of any module consists of two main parts:

 -# general configuration settings, and 
 -# settings related to the specific module.

Only the second part varies from one module to another. For instance, let's have a look at the configuration file `alamASR_AKDQFF.cfg` located in `examples` directory. The file contains:
\includelineno  alamASR_AKDQFF.cfg
 
In the example above only the last four lines pertain to the specific configuration of the module. All the leading lines are related to the general configuration.

Basically, any line may include one or more _field_, possibly each one of different type. In this manual we follow a convention that field names are typeset with the \conffield{bold typewriter} fonts. A declaration of the field always involves giving its type, which can be one of: `path`, `string`, `logical`, `integer`  or `real` (trivial data types), or `srtensor`, `symsrtensor`, `realarray` (compound data types). In this manual we adapt convention that the type of the field is embraced within parentheses. For instance, the declaration \confdef{example_field,integer} defines a field of type `integer` called \conffield{example_field}.

Any _configuration option_ may belong to one of the two groups:
 -# single-line options: such options always occupy exactly one line in the configuration file. One or more fields per line is allowed. 
 -# multi-line options: depending on the values of the fields in the first line, __zero or more__ subsequent lines is read and interpreted. As in the case of the single-line options, any line may contain one or more fields. Depending on the content of the data fields, number of lines to be read may vary.

\subsection range_option  Options for expressing range of values
There is a special kind of option that is used for defining ranges of values. The range option is always multi-line. The first line always contains a keyword field of type `string`, which determines the interpretation of the subsequent lines. The following variants of the range option are available:
<ol>

<li><a name="option_discrete">discrete</a>: an explicitly specified set
of values.

The first line of the option contains the keyword `discrete`. The next line must contain one field \confdef{npoints,integer}. The subsequent line(s) must provide \conffield{npoints} real values.  
\par Example:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~{.cfg}
discrete             # Discrete data points
6                    # 6 real values follow
 6.0  7.0  8.0 
29.0 30.0 31.0  
~~~~~~~~~~~~~
\attention Comment lines are <em>not allowed</em> in certain types of data lines.

\attention Comment lines are <em>not allowed</em> if they break a single data line containing multiple values into two or more lines.
</li>
<li><a name="option_uniform">uniform</a>: uniformly distributed points within a closed interval. Note that the endpoints of the interval do belong to the range. \anchor option_uniform

The first line of the option contains the keyword `uniform`. The next line must contain three fields: \confdef{range_begin,real}  \confdef{range_end,real}  \confdef{range_step,real}. Note that unless the length of the interval is the multiple of \conffield{range_step}, the last step can be shorter. Please read <a href="#hint_even_range">the hint below </a> how to ensure even distribution of points inside the range.
\par Example:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~{.cfg}
uniform          # uniformly distributed points
0.0 180.0 40.0   # range [0,180], consisting of: 0.0 40.0 80.0 120.0 160.0 and 180.0
~~~~~~~~~~~~~
</li>
<li><a name="option_biased">biased</a>: non-uniformly distributed points, with the density higher at one of the endpoints. 

The first line contains the keyword `biased`. The second line shall contain three fields: \confdef{range_begin,real}  \confdef{range_end,real}  \confdef{ratio,real}. The third line must contain a single field:   \confdef{number_of_points,integer}.

The meaning of the first two fields is identical as in the `uniform` range. The  field \conffield{ratio} is a non-negative real value, which denotes a ratio between the largest and the smallest step between the points within the range. If (0.0 &lt; \conffield{ratio} &lt; 1.0) then the smallest steps are located at the beginning of the range; otherwise (\conffield{ratio} &gt; 1.0) the refined steps appear at the end of the range. 
\par Example:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~{.cfg}
# Unevenly distributed points within range [0,180], 
# with single bias controlled by ratio=4.0
# This results in points: 0.0 64.04 109.33 141.35 163.99 and 180.0
#  
biased         # unevenly distributed points, single bias  
0.0 180.0 4.0  # range_begin=0, range_end=180, ratio=4.0 
5              # number_of_points=5
~~~~~~~~~~~~~
</li>
<li>double-biased: \anchor option_doublebiased non-uniformly distributed points, with the density either higher or lower at the endpoints. Basically, the double bias gives a sequence of points that would result from two biased ranges stitched together, with the stitching point located at the half point of the interval [\conffield{range_begin},\conffield{range_end}] and the \conffield{ratio} of the second range being a reciprocal of the \conffield{ratio} in the first range. As a consequence, the highest density of points shall be located around the centre of the interval if \conffield{ratio} &gt; 1.0. Conversely, if (0.0 &lt;  \conffield{ratio} &lt;  1.0) the most refined steps will be put next to the endpoints of the range. Note that the mid-point of the interval is always included in the sequence of points defined by the double-biased range.   

The keyword `doublebiased` must be given in the first line.  The second line shall contain three fields: \confdef{range_begin,real}  \confdef{range_end,real}  \confdef{ratio,real}. The third line must contain a single field:   \confdef{number_of_points,integer}.
The meaning of the first two fields is identical as in the `uniform` range. The  field \conffield{ratio} is a non-negative real value that defines a ratio between the largest and the smallest step within the first half of the interval and, at the same time it defined a ratio between the smallest and the largest step in the second half of the interval.
\par Example:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~{.cfg}
# Unevenly distributed points within range [0,180], 
# with double bias controlled by ratio=4.0
# This results in points: 0.0 51.43 77.14 90.0 102.86 128.57 and 180.0
doublebiased     # unevenly distributed points, double-biased
0.0 180.0 4.0    # range_begin=0, range_end=180, ratio=4.0
7                # number_of_points=7
~~~~~~~~~~~~~
</li>
<li>multi-biased: \anchor option_multibiased non-uniformly distributed points, with arbitrarily selected biased sub-ranges.

The option requires the keyword `multibiased` in the first line. The second line consists of one field \confdef{range_begin,real}, and the third line contains one field \confdef{number_of_ranges,integer}. The value of the field \conffield{number_of_ranges} defines how many sub-ranges shall be created; it must be at least 1. For every subrange two lines are read. each consisting of three fields: \confdef{subrange_endpoint,real} \confdef{subrange_ratio,real} \confdef{subrange_number_of_points,integer}. The endpoint of every subrange becomes a start point of the next subrange, except for the last one which terminates the range. The rules for the \conffield{subrange_ratio} and \conffield{subrange_number_of_points} are identical to those for \conffield{ratio} and \conffield{number_of_points} in the <a href="#option_biased">biased range</a>, respectively.
\par Example:
The following `multibiased` range definition  is equivalent to the example of double-biased range described in the <a href="#option_doublebiased">previous section</a>.
~~~~~~~~~~~~~{.cfg}
# Unevenly distributed points within range [0,180], 
# with multiple biases
# This results in points: 0.0 51.43 77.14 90.0 102.86 128.57 and 180.0
multibiased      # unevenly distributed points, multi-biased
0.0              # range_begin=0.0
2                # number_of_ranges=2
90.0  4.0        # subrange 1: from 0.0 to 90.0
3                # subrange 1: subrange_number_of_points=3
180.0 0.25       # subrange 2: from 90.0 to 180.0
3                # subrange 2: subrange_number_of_points=3
~~~~~~~~~~~~~
</li>
</ol>

<a name="hint_even_range">\par Hint</a>
A uniform range can be defined by explicitly giving the step between the points. It has a shortcoming that depending on the ratio between the step and the length of the range, the last step can be selected shorter.  However, it is possible to define a uniform range with evenly distributed points. To do so, one can declare a `biased` range with the  \conffield{ratio} equal to 1.0 and the specific number of points. 

\subsection euler_option  Options for expressing Euler angles.

There is another commonly used type of configuration option, which allows expressing either a rotation angle of material axes or a reference frame. The option uses Euler angles in Bunge notation. This is always a single-line option, comprising three fields of the type `real`: \f$ \phi_1 \f$, \f$ \Phi \f$ and \f$ \phi_2 \f$.

